-- The New Zealand rates rebate as the deck "20190514 Multilateral Isomorphism
-- in L4" encodes it: the FIXED form.
-- It is rates-rebate-buggy.l4 with one change, the clamp on the income excess,
-- which is the deck's "Bug fix by @Br3nda and @Verbman".
-- No section of the Act is cited here, because none has been checked against
-- the Act; the formula and the parameters are the deck's.
--
-- The deck's worked number: rates 1000, income 20000, no dependants pays 560
-- here and 630 in the buggy form.
--
-- The outer `max 0` follows the deck's later charts and the OpenFisca code's
-- `clip(rebate, 0, ...)`; the deck's L4 fragment has none.
--
-- The helpers are top-level rules, one for each clause of the rule as the
-- deck words it.
-- (Written when `l4 prove` still crashed on a WHERE binding that takes no
-- parameters; #784 has since fixed that, and the helpers stay as they are.)
IMPORT prelude

GIVETH A NUMBER
`income threshold` MEANS 25180

GIVETH A NUMBER
`additional per dependant` MEANS 500

GIVETH A NUMBER
`initial contribution` MEANS 160

GIVETH A NUMBER
`maximum allowable` MEANS 630

GIVEN dependants IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`allowable income` dependants MEANS
  `income threshold` PLUS dependants TIMES `additional per dependant`

GIVEN rates IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rates excess` rates MEANS max 0 (rates MINUS `initial contribution`)

-- $1 for each $8 by which the income exceeds the allowable income.
-- FIXED: clamped at 0 before the FLOOR, so a low income is not a bonus.
GIVEN income     IS A NUMBER
      dependants IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`income excess` income dependants MEANS
  FLOOR ((max 0 (income MINUS `allowable income` dependants)) DIVIDED BY 8)

GIVEN rates      IS A NUMBER
      income     IS A NUMBER
      dependants IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rebate` rates income dependants MEANS
  max 0
    (min rates
      (min (((2 TIMES `rates excess` rates) DIVIDED BY 3) MINUS `income excess` income dependants)
           `maximum allowable`))

-- decided by evaluation: the deck's worked number
#ASSERT `rebate` 1000 20000 0 EQUALS 560

-- decided by evaluation: a cell of the deck's chart above the allowable
-- income, where the clamp does nothing, so both forms agree
#ASSERT `rebate` 1000 26000 0 EQUALS 458

§ `Any ratepayer`
    GIVEN rates      IS A NUMBER
          income     IS A NUMBER
          dependants IS A NUMBER
          later      IS A NUMBER

-- proved: the rule's own words, as the deck renders them: "two-thirds of
-- the amount by which the rates exceed $160, reduced by ...", so a
-- reduction cannot lift the rebate above two-thirds of the rates excess
#ASSERT `rebate` rates income dependants AT MOST (2 TIMES `rates excess` rates) DIVIDED BY 3

-- proved: no bonus for a low income, so a ratepayer below the allowable
-- income gets no more than one exactly at it
#ASSERT income AT MOST `allowable income` dependants IMPLIES `rebate` rates income dependants AT MOST `rebate` rates (`allowable income` dependants) dependants

-- proved: the rebate does not rise when income rises, `later` being any
-- income at least as large as `income`
-- The buggy form satisfies this as well, so it cannot find that bug: see
-- rates-rebate-no-floor.l4.
#ASSERT later AT LEAST income IMPLIES `rebate` rates later dependants AT MOST `rebate` rates income dependants
